---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Command from "../components/Command.astro";
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
import Demo from "../components/Demo.astro";
import Artifact from "../components/Artifact.astro";

const install =
  "curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/achevalier-dev/orgami/main/bootstrap.sh | bash";

const keys = [
  ["tab", "Move between the map, the repositories, the notes and the recaps"],
  ["type", "Filter the list"],
  ["enter", "Print whatever is under the cursor into your scrollback, to copy or pipe"],
  ["ctrl-o", "Open the repository on GitHub"],
  ["ctrl-n", "Write a note against the repository you are reading"],
];

const facts = [
  "MIT",
  "macOS · Linux · WSL · Windows",
  "Claude Code plugin, or any MCP client",
  "2 model calls a week",
];
---

<Base
  title="orgami"
  description="Map every repository a GitHub organization has, recap what it merges, and hand all of it to your coding agent before you type."
>
  <Section id="top" first>
    <p class="mb-5 font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.19em] text-muted uppercase">
      org + origami — folding a flat sheet of repositories into a shape you can see
    </p>

    <div class="prose-col">
      <h1
        class="mb-[22px] text-[clamp(30px,4.6vw,49px)] leading-[1.1] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]"
      >
        Know the organization before you touch its code.
      </h1>

      <p class="mb-[18px] text-[20px] leading-[1.55] text-ink-soft">
        orgami maps every repository a GitHub organization has — what each one is, how to run it,
        what it talks to, where it deploys — recaps the pull requests it merged each week, digests
        each day, and hands all of it to your coding agent before you type anything.
      </p>

      <p class="text-muted">
        One directory per company, so several clients sit side by side without mixing. No daemon,
        no database, no web app: bash, <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">gh</code>,
        <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">jq</code>,
        <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">fzf</code>,
        <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">gum</code>, a timer, and plain files that diff cleanly
        in git.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-[26px] max-w-[68ch]">
      <Command command={install} />
    </div>

    <div class="mt-[22px] flex flex-wrap gap-x-[18px] gap-y-2 font-mono text-[11.5px] text-muted">
      {facts.map((f) => <span>{f}</span>)}
    </div>

    <Demo caption="Recorded against the public honojs organization. Every line came out of a committed file." />
  </Section>

  <Section id="evidence">
    <div class="prose-col">
      <h2 class="mb-2.5 text-[clamp(21px,2.5vw,27px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
        Nothing in it is a guess.
      </h2>
      <p>
        The scan reads what is committed — nothing is inferred by a model. Every edge in the map
        carries the <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">file:line</code> it was read from, so any
        claim can be opened and checked. When the scan cannot see something it says so instead of
        inventing it: a missing edge means “not found in committed configuration”, never
        “not connected”.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="term mt-[30px]">
      <pre><span class="a">▪ gum</span>  <span class="c">repo</span>

  <span class="c">language     </span>Go
  <span class="c">description  </span>A tool for glamorous shell scripts

<span class="c">REFERENCES</span>
  bubbletea                     <span class="c">write/write.go:18</span>
  glamour                       <span class="c">go.mod:11</span>
  lipgloss                      <span class="c">write/write.go:19</span>

<span class="c">USES</span>
  <span class="t">docker</span>                        <span class="c">Dockerfile</span>
  <span class="t">github-actions</span>                <span class="c">.github/workflows</span>

<span class="c">REFERENCED BY</span>
  vhs                           <span class="c">examples/README.md:5</span>
  homebrew-tap                  <span class="c">gum.rb:13</span></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-col">
      <p class="src">lib/scan.sh — every edge is emitted with its evidence argument, or not at all</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="makes">
    <div class="prose-col">
      <h2 class="mb-2.5 text-[clamp(21px,2.5vw,27px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
        What it makes.
      </h2>
      <p>
        Five artifacts, all markdown and JSON on disk, all committed to a docs repository you
        choose. Where a model is involved it writes prose only —
        <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">jq</code> computes every number and hands it over, because
        a model asked to both count and narrate does neither reliably.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div
      class="mt-[34px] grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-[4px] border border-rule bg-rule md:grid-cols-2"
    >
      <Artifact title="The map" kind="repo">
        Deploy tooling, servers, backing services, frameworks, run and test commands, routes, and
        which repositories reference which. One <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">graph.json</code>;
        the architecture doc, the diagrams and the terminal browser all render from it.
      </Artifact>

      <Artifact title="The weekly recap" kind="host">
        Every pull request the organization merged, grouped by why it happened rather than by
        repository, with a section on how the team worked — review concentration, cycle time, how
        much merged with nobody else reading it.
      </Artifact>

      <Artifact title="The daily digest" kind="tool">
        What merged, what opened, what is waiting on someone, and commits with no pull request
        behind them — the half a merge-based recap cannot see. A quiet day writes no file at all.
      </Artifact>

      <Artifact title="Team notes" kind="service">
        The cause someone found at 2am, the reason the obvious fix does not work. Screened for
        credentials and for any other client on the machine before they are written, and again
        before they are pushed.
      </Artifact>

      <Artifact title="Runbooks and decisions" kind="muted" wide>
        Per repository: how to run it, which workflow ships it and on what trigger, where its alerts
        go, what it drags with it when it changes. Alongside them,
        <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">DECISIONS.md</code> — what was adopted, dropped or
        rejected, mined from merged pull requests one week at a time, every bullet carrying its pull
        request.
      </Artifact>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-col">
      <p
        class="mt-[30px] border-l-2 border-accent bg-surface px-5 py-4 text-[16px] text-ink-soft"
      >
        <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">A day is not a small week.</strong> Most of a day's work
        has not merged yet, so the digest reads four sources instead of one — and skips the day entirely
        when nothing landed. A digest that says “nothing happened” every weekend teaches people to stop
        opening it.
      </p>
      <p class="src">lib/daily.jq — the numbers · prompts/daily.md — what the model may narrate</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="screen">
    <div class="prose-col">
      <h2 class="mb-2.5 text-[clamp(21px,2.5vw,27px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
        One screen for all of it.
      </h2>
      <p>
        <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">orgami view</code> is four tabs over the same data: the
        map, the repositories, the team's notes and the recaps. The list is on the left, the evidence
        is in the pane on the right, and the header says how stale the map is and whether this week has
        a recap yet.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src="/tui.png"
      alt="The orgami terminal browser: a header with the organization and map age, tabs for Map, Repos, Notes and Recaps, a list of repositories on the left, and a repository card on the right showing run commands, linked repositories, deploy targets and endpoints, each with the file and line it came from."
      caption="The Repos tab. Every line in the right-hand pane carries the file it was read from."
    />

    <table class="mt-7 w-full border-collapse text-[15px]">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th
            class="border-b border-rule py-2.5 pr-0 pl-0 text-left font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.14em] text-muted uppercase"
            >Key</th
          >
          <th
            class="border-b border-rule py-2.5 text-left font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.14em] text-muted uppercase"
            >What it does</th
          >
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          keys.map(([key, what]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="w-[1%] border-b border-rule py-2.5 pr-[26px] font-mono text-[12.5px] whitespace-nowrap text-accent">
                {key}
              </td>
              <td class="border-b border-rule py-2.5 text-ink-soft">{what}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="prose-col">
      <p class="src">
        lib/tui.sh — every pane is a subcommand, so orgami query prints exactly what the preview shows
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="agents">
    <div class="prose-col">
      <h2 class="mb-2.5 text-[clamp(21px,2.5vw,27px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
        Your agent already has it.
      </h2>
      <p>
        With the plugin installed there is nothing to run. Opening Claude Code in a mapped checkout
        puts the repository's stack, its exact run and test commands, the repositories it talks to and
        the team's notes into the session before you type. Outside a mapped repository it prints
        nothing at all.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="term mt-[30px]">
      <pre><span class="c">orgami — winit (winitapp), map from 2026-08-17</span>

<span class="b">WinIt-backend</span> <span class="c">— TypeScript, Agenda jobs, Express, Parse SDK, Parse Server</span>

  <span class="c">build:</span> tsc -p tsconfig.build.json
  <span class="c">test:</span>  cross-env NODE_ENV=test ... jest --silent --coverage test/*

<span class="c">linked repos:</span>
  calls <span class="a">-&gt;</span> Attorney-Portal
  changes-with <span class="a">&lt;-</span> Close-SMS-Report

<span class="t">team notes on this repo:</span>
- Parse Dashboard config lives in WinIt-ParseDashboard/index.js, not the fork …</pre>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-col">
      <p class="mt-[26px]">
        Cursor reaches the same parity through its session hook. Anything that speaks MCP — opencode,
        Windsurf, Zed, Codex, VS Code — gets the map as tools. Anything that reads
        <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">AGENTS.md</code> can have the context written into the
        repository itself, as a marked block that is rewritten and never overwrites what you wrote
        around it.
      </p>
      <p class="src">hooks/session-context.sh · lib/mcp.py · lib/agents.sh</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="install">
    <div class="prose-col">
      <h2 class="mb-2.5 text-[clamp(21px,2.5vw,27px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
        Install it in a minute.
      </h2>
      <p>
        One command installs what is missing, puts <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">orgami</code> on
        your PATH, and wires up Claude Code and Cursor if they are on the machine.
        <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">--dry-run</code> prints every step without touching anything.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-[30px] grid max-w-[68ch] gap-3.5">
      <Command command={install} />
      <Command command="gh auth login" />
      <Command command="orgami init" />
    </div>

    <div class="prose-col">
      <p class="mt-[26px]">
        In Claude Code, <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">/plugin marketplace add achevalier-dev/orgami</code>
        brings the skill, the commands and the session hook. A colleague who wants the map without
        scanning anything runs <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">orgami join</code> — it finds the
        repository that already holds one.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-[26px] border-l-2 border-accent bg-surface px-5 py-4 text-[16px] text-ink-soft">
        <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">What it costs.</strong> Two model calls a week — the recap
        and the decision mining — plus one a weekday morning if you turn the digest on. Everything else is
        gh, git, jq and grep. <code class="font-mono text-[0.88em]">orgami report --stats-only</code> costs
        nothing at all.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-[26px]">
        The scan is pattern matching over committed files, so every organization it has never met is a
        gap in it. An organization it read wrong is the most useful bug report there is — there is a
        template for exactly that, and it needs no fix attached.
      </p>
      <p class="src">CONTRIBUTING.md — testing it needs no organization, no token and no model</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <footer class="border-t border-rule py-11 pb-[70px] font-mono text-[12.5px] text-muted">
    <div class="mb-5 flex flex-wrap gap-x-[26px] gap-y-2.5">
      <a href="https://github.com/achevalier-dev/orgami">Source</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/achevalier-dev/orgami#readme">Documentation</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/achevalier-dev/orgami/labels/good%20first%20issue">Good first issues</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/achevalier-dev/orgami/discussions">Discussions</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/achevalier-dev/orgami/releases/tag/v1.0.0">v1.0.0</a>
    </div>
    <div>MIT · built by Antoine Chevalier · org + origami</div>
  </footer>
</Base>
